331:## 2. Generation of out-of-sample centile scores using GAMLSS
335:Location Scale and Shape (GAMLSS) (Mikis Stasinopoulos &amp; Rigby, 2008) was used to estimate cross-sectional age-related trends from 100 studies, incorporating a reference dataset with over 75,000 scans of typically developing individuals. GAMLSS model specification and parameterisation was optimised to estimate non-linear growth curves, their confidence intervals and first derivatives, separately for males and females, allowing for random effects on the mean and higher-order moments of the outcome distributions. Model reliability was evaluated through cross-validation and bootstrap resampling procedures. Individualised centile scores (ranging from 0 to 1) were computed which benchmarked each scan in relation to average age-related trends, quantified  in  terms  of  relative  distance  from  the  median  of  age-normed  and  sex-stratified distributions  provided  by  the  reference  model.  This  approach  is  analogous  to  traditional anthropometric  growth  charts,  where  the  divergence  from  the  population  'norm'  of  each phenotype in each scan is quantified by its score on the distribution of phenotypic parameters in the  reference  sample  of  scans,  with  more  extreme  centile  scores  (close  to  0  or  1)  indicating greater divergence from the sample average (0.5).
337:Adopting a sex-stratified approach, the GAMLSS model included age, preprocessing pipeline, and  study  in  estimating  firstorder  (μ)  and  second -order  (σ)  distribution  parameters  of  a generalised gamma distribution using fractional polynomials to model nonlinear trends.
357:We note that some of the datasets included in our sample were also included in the original BrainCharts sample. We nonetheless generated out-of-sample centiles for our whole dataset, as the original openly available BrainCharts models did not separate individual sites in some of the large, combined datasets such as ABIDE and ADHD200, instead treating them as a single study. Thus,  using  the  original  centiles  for  these  studies  would  not  include  any  site  correction  or harmonisation. Instead, we first used ComBat and then GAMLSS, correcting for each site within these  datasets.  The  original  paper  (Bethlehem  et  al.  2022)  conducted  a  leave-one-study-out analysis, demonstrating that when removing a study from the sample and re-fitting the GAMLSS model, the new out-of-sample centiles for the removed study are essentially unchanged from the original in-sample centile scores ( r = ~0.99). We also calculated correlation values, using ABIDE as an example, between our out-of-sample centile scores and the in-sample centiles originally calculated, and found very high correlation values for the four main global phenotypes: r = 0.88 for GMV, r = 0.91 for WMV, r = 0.93 for sGMV, and r = 0.97 for ventricular volume. These are slightly lower than the ~0.99 correlation values reported in the original paper, likely due to the fact that in our out-of-sample calculations, we treated every ABIDE site as an individual study, and some of these sites were quite small, whereas the original Brain Chart models only treated ABIDE 1 and ABIDE 2 each as one site.
363:Before using GAMLSS to generate centile scores, we harmonised data using ComBat. This is more effective than other techniques such as controlling for site or scanner for several reasons. First,  ComBat  is  specifically  designed  to  effectively  remove  systematic  variations  in  data introduced by factors like different imaging sites or scanners, whereas controlling for site in a statistical  model  might  not  fully  capture  and  remove  these  batch  effects.  More  importantly, ComBat is known for its  ability  to  remove  unwanted  technical  variability  (batch  effects)  while preserving biological variability. This is important because variation from site and demographic or clinical effects due to cohort are difficult to disentangle; thus, controlling for site in a statistical model might inadvertently remove some genuine biological variations along with the site effects. ComBat is also more effective at controlling for non-linear batch effects, and has less risk of overfitting (Bayer, Dinga, et al., 2022; Bayer, Thompson, et al., 2022; Fortin et al., 2018).
503:Results of the analyses of non-ComBat harmonised centiles were almost identical to those using ComBat,  indicating  that,  despite  some  sites  having  small  sample  sizes  in  some  groups,  the models were largely stable and batch correction using ComBat did not improve that done by GAMLSS. Effect size maps looked visually almost identical, with the exception of a couple of differences in which regions reached significance. Correlations of unthresholded effect size maps between the two analyses were extremely high; the lowest was for CV for the main effect of autism in males only, where rho = 0.90, for all other analyses rho = 0.95 - 0.99. Analyses are displayed side by side to demonstrate the similarity between maps (Figure S1.2.1-S1.2.2)
571:We also compared 'raw' GMV values across sites, before and after ComBat, and note that even here, the differences are more apparent than for the centile scores. The GAMLSS model used for centile scoring also further normalises data across sites. While there were still some site effects remaining, it is impossible to know whether this is due to scanner differences or true biological variation and differences between study samples.
573:To examine the impact of ComBat site harmonisation we conducted an ANOVA to test the effect of  site  on  grey  matter  volume  and  found  that  while  the  effect  of  site  was  still  significant  after ComBat, the F value decreased substantially from F = 15.64 to F = 5.92. The effect of site was slightly  further  diminished  after  centilisation  using  GAMLSS,  but  was  essentially  the  same  if centiles were generated with (F = 3.90) or without (F = 3.64) ComBat, indicating that while ComBat does  attenuate  site  effects  in  the  raw  data,  it  does  not  seem  to  provide  additional  site harmonisation above and beyond the harmonisation provided with GAMLSS. This effect appears to be similar for other randomly selected regions (figure S1.2.3-S1.2.6).

