   329	We also examined associations between centile scores and IQ, and found only a few significant but weak correlations, for GMV, WMV, and total SA, all rho = 0.12-0.14. Mean CT and ventricular volume were not significantly correlated with IQ ( rho = 0.01-0.02, p&gt;0.05). IQ tends to be highly collinear with neurodevelopmental conditions and closely related to diagnostic status, in particular autism, as well as with sex differences (Loomes et al., 2017). Thus, controlling for IQ may remove biological variation related to the conditions themselves or confound results. As argued by Dennis and colleagues (Dennis et al., 2009), covarying for IQ can result in overcorrected or spurious outcomes. Additionally, we did not have IQ from every site, and those that we did have IQ for all used differing measures. Finally, many of the IQ measures used are likely not optimal for use in children with neurodevelopmental conditions and thus may not adequately capture the nuances of intellectual ability differences, for example, the spiky profile (i.e. pronounced areas of strength as well as difficulty) in these populations. Therefore, we did not feel that including IQ as a covariate in our analyses was optimal.
